{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "139",
    "case_summary": "A woman in her 30s with no relevant medical history was referred for a progressive bilateral nasal airway obstruction.\n\nEndoscopic examination found a voluminous, pulsatile, highly vascularized mass in both nasal cavities.\n\n{'Imaging Studies': {'Endoscopic View': {'Title': 'Endoscopic View of the Right Nasal Cavity', 'Description': 'A hypervascular and pulsatile tumor occupies almost the entire right nasal fossa'}, 'CT Scan': {'Description': 'Enhanced CT scan showed a hypervascular tumor in the nasal cavity, on the left thyroid, the fifth rib, and the L4 vertebra'}, 'MRI': {'Description': 'Confirmed a hypervascular tumor with anterior skull base extension (contrast enhancement after gadolinium injection, intermediate T2 signal)'}, 'Radioiodine Scintigraphy': {'Description': 'Found considerable uptake regarding rib and vertebral lesions. The sinonasal tumor had low uptake, indicative of thyroid tissue with low iodine avidity'}}, 'Laboratory Tests': {'Serum Thyroglobulin': '4432 ng/mL (significantly elevated)', 'Biopsy Results': {'Costal Biopsy': 'Pointed to VTC metastasis', 'Sinonasal Tumor Biopsy': 'Confirmed VTC metastasis'}}}\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Metastatic Papillary Thyroid Carcinoma (PTC)\nB. Metastatic Vascular Thyroid Carcinoma\nC. Radioiodine-refractory vesicular thyroid carcinoma (VTC) with sinonasal metastasis\nD. Metastatic Follicular Thyroid Carcinoma\n",
    "root": {
      "label": "Subacute Progressive Bilateral Nasal Airway Obstruction with Pulsatile and Vascularized Mass",
      "time_course": "subacute",
      "severity": "unspecified",
      "confidence": 0.8,
      "supporting_facts": [
        "progressive bilateral nasal airway obstruction",
        "endoscopic examination found a voluminous, pulsatile, highly vascularized mass in both nasal cavities",
